WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.640
Oh, hi there.

9
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:32.200
Every time I hear our little opening intro and I hear Creed playing, it makes me happy.

10
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.200
Me too.

11
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.020
So just starting with that.

12
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:40.180
But it's a nice way to start a moment, a day, a podcast for sure.

13
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:40.700
For sure.

14
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.280
Our podcast for sure.

15
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.160
Well, yes, that for sure.

16
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.160
You want to hear what we're talking about today?

17
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:47.720
Oh, I do.

18
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.400
I hope it's not a surprise to you.

19
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:50.120
I hope not.

20
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.600
It is The Whale.

21
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.920
It is Season 9, Episode 7, written by Carrie Kemper and directed by Rodman Flender.

22
00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.380
Do you remember Rodman?

23
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.100
I do.

24
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.180
He was so fun.

25
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.160
So fun.

26
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.140
And I remember being like, Rodman, come back.

27
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:04.820
I know.

28
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.600
He's directed a ton of television and he has a very famous nephew, Timothee Chalamet.

29
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.140
Can you believe it?

30
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:13.620
Yeah.

31
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.320
We didn't realize that back then.

32
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:15.920
No.

33
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.180
But Timothee Chalamet wasn't Timothee Chalamet back then.

34
00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:19.480
I know.

35
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.540
Rodman, we would be asking for all the deets on your nephew.

36
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.560
What a talent.

37
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.040
Like the annoying aunts.

38
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:26.660
We would.

39
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:27.000
Yes.

40
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.620
We would be like, what project is he in now?

41
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:29.880
Yes.

42
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.360
What's he up to?

43
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.580
I noticed he changed his hair and I like it.

44
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.200
I think it frames his face nicely.

45
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.780
Well, I love his fashion on the red carpet.

46
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.100
Yes.

47
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.200
He takes risks.

48
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.040
He takes chances and he sticks with it.

49
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.020
We love him, Rodman.

50
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:41.720
We love him.

51
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.800
Rodman's listening to this right now and he's like, thank God.

52
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:46.480
Oh, my gosh.

53
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:47.320
All right.

54
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.640
Here is your summary.

55
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.340
Dwight is tasked with securing a new client, so the ladies in the office attempt to teach

56
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.060
him how to sell to women.

57
00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.200
Because this new client is a woman.

58
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.800
That's all we know about her at firSt.

59
00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.440
Yeah.

60
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.280
A woman.

61
00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:08.040
Oscar is going to help Angela investigate her hunch that the senator might be having an affair.

62
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.680
And finally, Jim's telecommuting is not quite working out.

63
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.380
No.

64
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.960
He's trying to take a phone call.

65
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.080
This reminds me of trying to have a private conversation when you have a house full of kids.

66
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.920
You like duck into nooks and crannies, but everyone still finds you.

67
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:25.420
Yeah.

68
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:25.800
Yeah.

69
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.800
We got a lot of mail about that particular storyline.

70
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.320
I can't wait for us to discuss.

71
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:30.880
Ooh, good.

72
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:31.980
All right.

73
00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.320
Fast fact number one.

74
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.280
This episode was written by Carrie Kemper, who is the sister of Ellie Kemper.

75
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.020
And Carrie also plays Jan's assistant, Molly.

76
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.020
I thought she did so great as Molly.

77
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:45.620
So good.

78
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.200
I want to know more about Molly.

79
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.200
Well, I reached out to Carrie to ask her some questions about this episode.

80
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.200
And I just love her.

81
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:54.800
I mean, I love these Kemper gals.

82
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:55.160
I know.

83
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.140
They're such good gals.

84
00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.500
Carrie told me that the original pitch for this episode was very different from the final script.

85
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.360
It was originally called Land Jan.

86
00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:10.700
They knew that they were going to have Jan come back and that she was going to be someone we were pitching to.

87
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:20.480
And in the original pitch, Pam comes into work at the beginning of the day and she is so bummed because she ran into Jan at the grocery store and promised to go to lunch with her.

88
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:27.960
But then Pam realizes that if she makes Dwight go with her, that Jan will probably end the lunch early because Jan hates Dwight.

89
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.480
So Dwight and Pam go to lunch with Jan.

90
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.080
And while they're there, they realize that she wants to give them her business.

91
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:35.660
Okay.

92
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.100
And they start competing for who can sell Jan more paper and it becomes very clear that Dwight is horrible at selling to women.

93
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:43.920
Okay.

94
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:53.140
So Carrie said that Greg read the pitch and he zeroed in on one detail, which was that Dwight is terrible at selling to women.

95
00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.540
And Greg said, that's your story.

96
00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.160
That's what it should be.

97
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.240
And also, Jan should be a surprise.

98
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.620
It should be a reveal.

99
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.500
I 100% agree.

100
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.820
This is the genius of Greg, right?

101
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.720
I know, that he could look at that pitch and then find the two things that were going to make it zing.

102
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:10.300
Yes.

103
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.840
So they changed the title to The Whale and here we are.

104
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.960
Carrie also said that the Oscar Angela Senator storyline was an absolute dream.

105
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:29.820
She said the minute the writers pitched that and decided that that was going to be the storyline for this season, the whole story just kind of wrote itself.

106
00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.740
I have been so excited to get to these episodes because of my whole entire time on The Office.

107
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.820
I know I had amazing scenes with Rain and we got to play out the Dwight and Angela romance.

108
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:44.400
But these scenes with Oscar are some of my absolute favorites.

109
00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:51.900
And, you know, you guys remember Oscar and I met doing sketch comedy like, you know, my gosh, 20 years ago.

110
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.680
And to be able to do these scenes with him was just pure joy.

111
00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:56.840
We were giddy.

112
00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:57.680
It was so fun.

113
00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.000
Carrie said that the two of you together is absolute magic.

114
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.860
And she said that she felt like this particular beat of the story of you guys watching this yoga class.

115
00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:17.300
She said she felt like she like won the lottery because this was her particular favorite beat of the storyline and she got it.

116
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:18.240
Thanks, Carrie.

117
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:18.900
I know.

118
00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.840
Well, you know, I also asked Carrie how she got her job on The Office because we got a lot of mail.

119
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.100
People were curious if Ellie had anything to do with it.

120
00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:36.180
And Carrie said that Ellie kind of did, that Ellie had been on the show for about a year and she was sitting with Claire Scanlon one day.

121
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:43.820
And Claire told her that Greg and Paul were setting some meetings for new potential female writers for season seven.

122
00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.340
And Ellie said, my sister is a TV writer.

123
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.680
But the thing was, was that Carrie was an aspiring TV writer.

124
00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.380
She'd never actually had a job writing for television.

125
00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.220
She had been the editor of her college humor magazine, but she was at the very beginning of her career.

126
00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.580
But she had three spec scripts.

127
00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.720
Do people know what spec scripts are?

128
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.460
I think we've shared about it.

129
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.240
They're like a script that you write as a sample of your work, of what you can do.

130
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:10.720
Yeah.

131
00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.700
So you'll pick a show that's already on television and then you'll write an episode of it.

132
00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.860
So she had written spec scripts of the Sarah Silverman program.

133
00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.300
She did one for 30 Rock and she did one for Modern Family.

134
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.700
And so Claire agreed to pass on her Modern Family spec script to Paul.

135
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:32.740
And Paul read it and he called her in.

136
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:34.100
He loved it.

137
00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.240
He called her in for a meeting.

138
00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.420
And Carrie was like, oh, he's just being nice because my sister's on the show.

139
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.560
And listen to what her mom said.

140
00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.120
I love their mom.

141
00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.940
Her mom said, do you think he has time to be nice?

142
00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.180
Oh, yeah.

143
00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.560
This is a real meeting.

144
00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:51.420
You own it.

145
00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.000
You got yourself in the door with this good script.

146
00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:55.200
Good momming.

147
00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:55.900
I like that.

148
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.480
So Carrie went to the meeting and she said after the meeting, he asked for another writing sample.

149
00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.540
So she sent him a one-act play that she had written and she got hired a week later.

150
00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.480
That's such a great story.

151
00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.180
She said The Office was her favorite TV show.

152
00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.420
It was her first job writing on television.

153
00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:18.300
But, you know, she has gone on to write on some of seriously the best stuff on TV.

154
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:19.780
Silicon Valley.

155
00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:20.700
Oh, man.

156
00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.300
Beef.

157
00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:22.040
Oh.

158
00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.300
The Rehearsal.

159
00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:28.440
And she's currently co-executive producer of The Curse, which is now streaming on Paramount+.

160
00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.580
There you go.

161
00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:30.600
I love that story.

162
00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.680
I also just love the bond of sisterhood.

163
00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.160
That Ellie, without any hesitation, was like, my sister's a writer.

164
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:36.800
Yeah.

165
00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:38.140
So great.

166
00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:38.680
I know.

167
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.240
All the women in that family helped Carrie, right?

168
00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:42.540
Yes.

169
00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:46.120
Like, have the courage, the confidence, and the booSt.

170
00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:47.180
I love it.

171
00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:48.220
All right.

172
00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:49.480
Fast fact number two.

173
00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:55.380
Another big storyline in this episode, which I failed to mention in my summary, is that all

174
00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.420
the men in The Office are growing mustaches.

175
00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:58.800
Yes, they are.

176
00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.940
Do you say mustache or moustache?

177
00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.080
Who says moustache?

178
00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:04.060
I realize.

179
00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:05.160
That's like a Muppet.

180
00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.440
A Muppet says moustache.

181
00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:07.000
You know what I realize?

182
00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:07.820
Is that from Sesame Street?

183
00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.720
No, that's how I...

184
00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.140
Is that how you say moustache is one of your animals?

185
00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:13.620
Yes.

186
00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.260
That's how Sunny says moustache.

187
00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:15.600
Okay, you guys know.

188
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.280
Because he has a little white moustache.

189
00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.620
Jenna does the voices of her pets.

190
00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:20.300
We all do.

191
00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.580
That's why I was like, I almost said moustache.

192
00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.380
And then as I said it out loud, I realized no one says that.

193
00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:26.920
Just Sunny.

194
00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:27.980
Just Sunny.

195
00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:31.540
Sunny would say, oh my God, you have a really nice moustache.

196
00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.040
And you're made up voice for him.

197
00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:34.420
Yeah.

198
00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:35.700
Anyway.

199
00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:36.640
All right.

200
00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:43.400
So all the men in the office are growing mustaches for Movember.

201
00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:46.420
Movember is an annual event.

202
00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:52.460
Every November, men grow a moustache to help raise awareness for men's health issues like

203
00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:53.400
prostate cancer.

204
00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.700
And a lot of people do fundraisers in conjunction with growing their moustache.

205
00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.840
We got a fan letter from Keith S.

206
00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:03.640
in Los Angeles who said, I work for Movember, and I worked

207
00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:09.380
there in 2012 when this episode was written, and I would love to answer any questions anyone

208
00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:13.980
may have about Movember or how we worked with the writers to make sure there was an accurate

209
00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.840
representation of our organization.

210
00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.040
Way to go, Keith.

211
00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.500
Keith, I love that you wrote in.

212
00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.400
He said he loves the podcast, and he's been waiting for this episode.

213
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:21.680
Ooh.

214
00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.860
So we did reach out to Keith.

215
00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:24.340
Mm-hmm.

216
00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:25.400
And here's what he said.

217
00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:29.980
He said the writing staff reached out and let them know that they were going to use Movember

218
00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.300
in a storyline, but they didn't share the script.

219
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.560
But they checked to make sure that they were allowed to use it in the way they were using

220
00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.660
it and wanted to make sure that they got it accurate.

221
00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:40.300
Mm-hmm.

222
00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:45.600
He also heard that some of the writers had participated in Movember, and I remember

223
00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:46.120
that, Ange.

224
00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:46.680
I do, too.

225
00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:52.260
So he said they were just thrilled that The Office talked about Movember correctly, and

226
00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.320
it was such a great public platform.

227
00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:59.620
He said the whole team had a watch party to watch the episode, and he said they still share

228
00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:03.960
a GIF of Toby saying, smile if you love men's prostates on Slack.

229
00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:09.960
Keith said the writers also sent over a cookie bouquet of mustache and office-themed

230
00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.620
cookies when the episode aired.

231
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:13.040
I love that.

232
00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:13.880
Isn't that so sweet?

233
00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:14.300
Mm-hmm.

234
00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.720
Keith did want to say that Movember has come a long way since The Whale.

235
00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:23.420
He said they always focused on prostate and testicular cancer, but they now also raise awareness

236
00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.300
and funds for men's mental health and suicide prevention.

237
00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:32.860
He said that since 2003, they have built a global men's health movement funding more than

238
00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:34.460
1,300 projects.

239
00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:39.800
And he said our listeners can learn more or donate at Movember.com.

240
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.880
Keith, I will put that in our stories with a swipe up.

241
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.020
And thanks so much for writing in.

242
00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.160
Yeah, thank you, Keith.

243
00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.220
Oh, one more thing.

244
00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:51.540
He did want to give a shout out to Lisa, Abby, and Caitlin for helping him with all these

245
00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:51.960
details.

246
00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:52.880
Awesome.

247
00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:53.920
All right.

248
00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.240
Fast fact number three.

249
00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.000
I'm very excited, everyone, because Angela has asked for fast fact number three.

250
00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.960
And we are now going to have a mailbag moment with Angela Kinsey.

251
00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:08.980
A mailbag moment.

252
00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:15.060
By the way, Cody, who works on Office Ladies, told us there's even more mail that we need

253
00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:15.660
